T15.01|Strategic financial planning translates business choices into multi-period revenue, margin, investment, cash and funding requirements. It asks whether the strategy is financially feasible and what must be true for it to create value. A plan should connect operational drivers rather than simply increase every line by a target percentage.~~Targets express ambition; forecasts express expected outcomes under assumptions. Keeping them distinct helps management identify the actions and resources needed to close a gap.|Strategic target: Desired long-term outcome.;;Financial forecast: Expected result under explicit drivers.;;Funding plan: Sources and timing supporting execution.|A growth plan needs ₹80 lakh investment and expects ₹40 cumulative operating cash before expansion payments. Opening usable cash is ₹15.~~Internal resources total ₹55.;;Funding gap is ₹25.;;Assess financing, sequencing or reduced investment before presenting the plan as feasible.~~A profitable strategy can still require external cash.~~Forecast uncertainty and contingency needs can enlarge the gap.|Translate strategy into operational drivers.;;Model all statements and cash dates.;;Connect gaps with specific actions and funding.
T15.02|An annual operating plan sets coordinated activity and resource commitments for the coming year. Revenue assumptions drive production, staffing, purchasing and cash requirements, while capacity and funding constraints feed back into feasible sales.~~A negotiated target is not automatically a reliable forecast. Preserve assumptions, owners and action plans so variance analysis can distinguish changed conditions from execution gaps.|Revenue plan: Customer, volume, price and mix assumptions.;;Resource plan: Staff, materials and capacity.;;Financial plan: Profit, investment and cash consequences.|A firm plans 1,000 units at ₹100 with variable cost ₹60 and fixed cost ₹30,000.~~Revenue target = ₹100,000.;;Contribution = 1,000 × ₹40 = ₹40,000.;;Operating profit = ₹10,000 before other items.~~The plan's profit depends on selling the assumed volume at the assumed net price.~~Capacity, collections and inventory require separate schedules even when this earnings plan balances.|Assign driver ownership.;;Reconcile departments and constraints.;;Separate target commitments from current expectations.
T15.03|A master budget combines departmental plans into a consistent company view. Sales, production, purchasing, payroll, capital expenditure and cash schedules should agree on activity, timing and resource use. Independent departmental estimates can otherwise double-count savings or omit shared requirements.~~The consolidated budget should reconcile profit with changes in assets, obligations and cash. A departmental spending limit alone is not a complete financial plan.|Operating budgets: Sales and resource consumption.;;Capital budget: Long-lived asset spending.;;Cash budget: Dated receipts, payments and funding.|Sales budget is 1,000 units. Opening stock is 100 and target closing stock 150.~~Required production = sales 1,000 + closing 150 − opening 100 = 1,050 units.;;Purchasing and labour plans should support 1,050, not 1,000.;;Cash payments follow supplier and payroll terms separately.~~Inventory targets connect the sales and production budgets.~~Waste, lead times and capacity can add further requirements.|Reconcile physical quantities first.;;Integrate departmental timing.;;Check profit, balance sheet and cash consistency.
T15.04|Incremental budgeting starts from an existing base and adjusts it. Zero-based budgeting justifies activities from their purpose and required resources. Activity-based budgeting estimates the volume of cost-driving activities and the resources they consume. Each involves different effort and information needs.~~Zero-based does not mean every necessary cost should be cut; activity-based does not guarantee the chosen drivers capture complexity. Select the method for the decision and data available.|Incremental: Adjust the prior spending base.;;Zero-based: Reassess the justification for activities.;;Activity-based: Budget resources from activity quantities.|Last-year processing cost was ₹100 lakh. This year 10,000 cases require ₹800 each plus ₹10 lakh fixed support.~~Incremental 10% budgeting would give ₹110.;;Activity-based estimate is ₹80 + ₹10 = ₹90.;;Investigate the discrepancy rather than assuming either method is automatically correct.~~Different methods expose different assumptions.~~Service quality, capacity and hidden complexity may change the activity-based estimate.|State the budgeting logic.;;Validate resource and activity assumptions.;;Balance review effort with decision importance.
T15.05|A flexible budget recalculates expected costs and results for actual activity, helping separate volume effects from efficiency or price effects. A rolling forecast updates expectations and extends the planning horizon as new periods arrive.~~Updating a forecast should not erase the original commitment used for accountability. Keep original budget, current forecast and actual results separately so learning remains possible.|Static budget: Original planned activity.;;Flexible budget: Expectations at actual activity.;;Rolling forecast: Updated outlook with an extended horizon.|Budget variable cost is ₹6 per unit for 100 units. Actual output is 120 and actual variable cost ₹750.~~Static budget cost is ₹600.;;Flexible budget cost is ₹720.;;Actual exceeds flexible cost by ₹30; the other ₹120 difference reflects higher volume.~~Comparing only with the static budget would overstate the efficiency problem.~~Price and usage drivers can further explain the ₹30.|Separate volume from execution.;;Retain original and revised forecasts.;;Update drivers with evidence and owners.
T15.06|Forecast schedules translate sales, input prices, headcount and asset plans into financial lines. Headcount cost depends on start dates, compensation and attrition; capex depends on commissioning and payment timing. A single growth rate often misses these mechanisms.~~Distinguish committed from discretionary spending and gross hiring from net headcount. Revenue forecasts need demand and capacity support; assumptions should reconcile across schedules.|Revenue driver: Quantity × net price.;;Headcount driver: Active staff × period compensation.;;Capex driver: Asset quantity, cost and payment dates.|Ten new employees each cost ₹10 lakh annually but join halfway through the year. Assume no other compensation costs.~~Full-year run-rate cost is ₹100 lakh.;;Current-year cost is ₹50 under the half-year service assumption.;;Next year's opening cost base includes the full ₹100 if all remain.~~A hiring plan's annualised run rate differs from current-period expense.~~Recruitment fees, benefits and attrition can change actual cost.|Model start dates and activity.;;Separate run rates from period amounts.;;Link expense, accrual and cash schedules.
T15.07|Variance analysis compares actual performance with a defined expectation and decomposes the gap into drivers. Price, quantity, efficiency and mix effects help identify action, while timing or accounting differences may explain changes without operational failure.~~A favourable cost variance can result from lower activity or neglected maintenance. Interpret variance with service, quality and cash rather than assuming every underspend is beneficial.|Price variance: Rate or unit-cost difference.;;Usage or efficiency variance: Resource quantity difference for activity.;;Volume variance: Actual activity differs from plan.|Budget material cost is 100 units at ₹5; actual is 110 at ₹6. Use quantity effect at budget price and price effect at actual quantity.~~Total variance = ₹660 − ₹500 = ₹160.;;Quantity effect = 10 × ₹5 = ₹50.;;Price effect = 110 × ₹1 = ₹110.~~The decomposition reconciles the full overspend.~~Different conventions allocate interaction differently; state the method and compare usage with actual output.|Choose a consistent baseline.;;Reconcile driver effects.;;Assess controllability, timing and operational consequences.
T15.08|Standard costing compares actual resource use with defined standards. Activity-based costing allocates resource cost using activities and their drivers rather than only a broad volume base. Both are analytical models requiring realistic assumptions.~~A product with low unit volume but many setups may consume more support than a simple units-based allocation suggests. Allocation improves visibility but does not establish that the assigned cost is avoidable.|Standard cost: Expected input quantity and rate.;;Volume allocation: Common costs assigned using output or another broad base.;;Activity-based cost: Costs assigned through specific activities.|Setup overhead is ₹100 lakh. Product A requires 80 setups and B 20, with no other setup drivers.~~Cost per setup = ₹1 lakh.;;Allocate ₹80 to A and ₹20 to B.;;Compare with a units-based allocation to identify complexity differences.~~Activity choice changes the estimated product cost.~~A single driver may still miss setup duration or complexity; validate causal resource use.|Define realistic standards.;;Select defensible activity drivers.;;Separate allocated from avoidable costs.
T15.09|Cost allocation assigns shared resources to departments or products. Responsibility centres define which results managers influence: costs, revenue, profit or investment. A performance measure should match actual authority and avoid creating incentives to shift costs or reject valuable activity.~~Common-cost allocations can support budgeting but distort decisions when treated as incremental cash. Evaluate fairness, controllability and reconciliation alongside arithmetic.|Cost centre: Primarily controls resource spending.;;Profit centre: Controls relevant revenue and cost.;;Investment centre: Also controls deployed capital.|Shared IT cost is ₹30 lakh, allocated by employee count: department A has 20 staff and B 10.~~A receives ₹20 and B ₹10 allocation.;;If B closes but total IT cost only falls ₹3, ₹7 of its allocation remains elsewhere.;;Use the ₹3 avoidable amount for the closure decision.~~Allocated responsibility differs from cash eliminated.~~Alternative allocation drivers can be more appropriate if usage differs.|Match metrics with authority.;;Explain allocation rules.;;Use avoidable costs for incremental choices.
T15.10|Relevant costing includes cash flows changing between alternatives. Make-or-buy analysis compares avoidable internal costs with purchase costs and opportunity effects. Existing allocated overhead that persists should not be counted as a saving.~~Consider quality, reliability, capacity and strategic dependence. Outsourcing can free scarce capacity for another profitable use, adding an opportunity benefit beyond direct cost comparison.|Avoidable cost: Disappears if the activity stops.;;Unavoidable cost: Persists despite the decision.;;Opportunity contribution: Benefit from alternative capacity use.|Internal unit cost is ₹10, including ₹3 unavoidable overhead. A supplier quotes ₹8, with no other differences.~~Relevant make cost is ₹7.;;Buying at ₹8 costs ₹1 more per unit than continuing.;;If outsourcing also frees capacity worth ₹2 contribution, the net comparison can favour buying by ₹1.~~The decision depends on actual changing resources and alternatives.~~Reliability and transition costs must be added when present.|Separate avoidable and allocated costs.;;Value feasible capacity alternatives.;;Assess quality, dependence and transition risk.
T15.11|Internal transfer pricing assigns value to transactions between responsibility centres. It affects measured divisional profit and incentives while generally cancelling in group consolidation. Economic decision pricing should consider external alternatives, capacity and opportunity costs.~~Tax transfer pricing is a separate legal topic; an internal managerial charge is not proof of compliance with cross-border tax rules. Design the internal mechanism to encourage useful group decisions.|Market-based transfer: Reference to external alternative prices.;;Cost-based transfer: Defined cost plus or without markup.;;Negotiated transfer: Divisions agree terms under policy.|Division A can supply a component at ₹6 incremental cost. Division B can buy externally for ₹9. Assume A has spare capacity.~~Group saving from internal supply is ₹3 per unit.;;An internal transfer price between ₹6 and ₹9 can share that saving.;;If A is capacity-constrained, include contribution forgone from external sales.~~A transfer charge redistributes divisional profit without changing the initial group saving.~~Tax and legal requirements need separate assessment.|Identify external alternatives.;;Assess capacity and opportunity cost.;;Align divisional incentives with group economics.
T15.12|Product-mix decisions allocate scarce resources among products. When one resource binds, contribution per unit of that resource can guide the choice under simple assumptions. Highest contribution per product unit may be wrong if it uses much more bottleneck capacity.~~Multiple constraints, demand limits and setup costs require explicit optimisation. Allocated accounting profit should not replace incremental contribution in a short-run capacity decision.|Unit contribution: Margin per item.;;Bottleneck contribution: Margin per scarce-resource unit.;;Constrained mix: Feasible combination under capacity and demand.|A contributes ₹12 and uses three machine hours; B contributes ₹10 and uses one. There are 100 hours and no demand limits.~~A earns ₹4 contribution per hour.;;B earns ₹10 per hour.;;The simplified optimum produces 100 B units for ₹1,000 contribution.~~The lower unit-margin product is better per scarce hour.~~Long-term capacity, customer commitments and setup effects can change the solution.|Identify the true bottleneck.;;Rank contribution per scarce unit.;;Include demand, service and additional constraints.
T15.13|A KPI tree links an outcome to measurable drivers, such as profit = customers × activity × contribution minus fixed costs. A dashboard should show definitions, trends, exceptions and decision ownership rather than a collection of unrelated attractive numbers.~~A leading indicator is useful only if it predicts or drives the desired result. Measure guardrails such as quality, churn and cash so a target cannot be met by shifting harm elsewhere.|Outcome KPI: Result such as cash or return.;;Driver KPI: Operational input influencing the outcome.;;Guardrail KPI: Prevents harmful optimisation.|A sales team tracks revenue, while management wants cash contribution.~~Add net price, contribution margin and collection days to the driver tree.;;Monitor returns and credit losses as guardrails.;;Assign actions when revenue grows but cash conversion deteriorates.~~A linked dashboard explains why the outcome changed and where to act.~~Correlation alone does not prove a chosen driver causes the result.|Define metrics and units.;;Link drivers to decisions.;;Use guardrails and accountable owners.
T15.14|Forecast accuracy compares estimates with outcomes under a stated error measure. Bias describes persistent over- or underprediction. Absolute error, percentage error and scale-weighted measures answer different questions, and percentage measures can fail near zero.~~Assess accuracy by horizon, segment and update date. A forecast revised immediately before the outcome is not comparable with a six-month-ahead forecast; retain point-in-time versions.|Absolute error: Currency or quantity difference.;;Percentage error: Error relative to a stated base.;;Bias: Directional tendency across forecasts.|Forecast revenue is ₹100 lakh and actual ₹80.~~Absolute error is ₹20.;;Absolute percentage error using actual as denominator is 20/80 = 25%.;;Error relative to forecast is 20/100 = 20%, a different convention.~~The denominator changes the reported accuracy measure.~~Review several forecasts and operational causes instead of declaring the model poor from one surprise.|State the error formula.;;Preserve dated forecasts and horizons.;;Analyse bias and errors by driver and segment.
T15.15|Finance business partnering turns analysis into decisions with operational colleagues. Board reporting should state the result, driver, implication, uncertainty and proposed action. Explain cash and capital consequences rather than presenting favourable earnings without funding context.~~A report should distinguish verified facts, assumptions and choices. Clear ownership and follow-up make analysis useful; dense tables alone do not ensure decision quality.|Performance explanation: What changed and why.;;Decision proposal: Options, economics and risks.;;Governance follow-up: Owner, milestone and evidence.|Sales beat plan by ₹20 lakh, but overdue receivables rise ₹25 and cash is below plan.~~Explain the credit and collection drivers alongside revenue.;;Quantify the funding gap and downside.;;Recommend specific collection or credit actions with accountable owners.~~The board receives a decision-focused picture rather than an isolated success headline.~~Avoid asserting fraud or customer failure without evidence.|Lead with the decision implication.;;Separate evidence from estimates.;;Assign actions and review dates.
